---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LevelPill from '../components/LevelPill.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { levels, type Level } from '../lib/views';

const order: Level[] = ['pictured', 'listed', 'reported'];
---
<Layout
  title="About HotelsViewer and how we check a view"
  description="HotelsViewer shows which hotel room category to book for a real view of a landmark. How we check each view, what our labels mean, and how the site makes money."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>About HotelsViewer</h1>
      <p class="dek measure">HotelsViewer answers one question: which hotel room do you book to actually get the view?</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose measure">
      <h2>Why the room name matters</h2>
      <p>A hotel next to a landmark can sell rooms that face it and rooms that face a courtyard, at the same address and often at a similar price. The difference usually sits in a few words of the room category name, like “High Floor”, “Balcony” or “Eiffel View”, and booking sites show those names in small print. We collect them, compare them, and tell you which category to book and which look-alikes to skip.</p>

      <h2 id="how-we-check">How we check a view</h2>
      <p>For every hotel we read the room categories on the hotel's own site and on booking sites, and we copy the names exactly as they are sold. Then we give the hotel one of three labels:</p>
    </div>

    <dl class="levels measure">
      {order.map((l) => (
        <div class="level">
          <dt><LevelPill level={l} /></dt>
          <dd>{levels[l].text}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <div class="prose measure">
      <p>Every entry shows the month of the check and the sources we used. Guides and travel sites are named when a detail comes from them, for example when a guide reports that a view is partial.</p>

      <h2>What we don't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We don't take payment from hotels to be listed, and a hotel that pays us nothing is listed the same way.</li>
        <li>We don't use photos we have no right to use. Our photos come with a free licence or from the people who hold the rights, and each one is credited where it appears.</li>
        <li>We don't promise a view. Hotels rename categories and assign rooms on the day, so check the category on the booking page before you pay.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <p>HotelsViewer is an independent project. It isn't owned by a hotel, a hotel group or a booking site.</p>

      <h2>How the site makes money</h2>
      <p>Some links may earn us a commission when you book, at no extra cost to you. It never changes which rooms we list. Read our <a class="text-link" href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> for the details.</p>

      <h2>Spotted a mistake?</h2>
      <p>Room names change. If you stayed in one of these rooms and the view was different, or a category has been renamed, write to <a class="text-link" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. We check every report and update the page.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .levels {
    margin: 8px 0 24px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .level {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 120px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 4px 20px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .level dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .level {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
